From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julien Etienne <julien.etienne@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 18:25:02 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] cpufreq: dt: set the OPP of a CPU with a supply at init

The regulator core counts a consumer's voltage only after the consumer
has asked for one. For cpufreq-dt that is the policy's first frequency
change. A policy that boots at a listed frequency makes no change at
init, so its CPUs run there with no voltage request.

On a rail that two policies share, as VPROC1 feeds both MT6797 Cortex-A53
clusters, the other policy can then lower the rail below what the first
one's clock needs. For example, CPU4-7 dropping to 325 MHz takes VPROC1
to 840 mV while CPU0-3 still run at the 897 MHz they booted at, which
needs 1000 mV.

When the CPU has a supply, set its OPP at init, at the current clock.
The OPP core applies a table in full the first time it is used, so this
registers the voltage even when the rate does not change. A clock at an
unlisted boot rate moves to the next OPP up here, where a failure fails
the policy, instead of in the core's initial frequency check, which
calls BUG_ON() on failure.
---
 drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq-dt.c | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq-dt.c b/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq-dt.c
index eec842afa..1712b730a 100644
--- a/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq-dt.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq-dt.c
@@ -102,6 +102,22 @@ static int cpufreq_init(struct cpufreq_policy *policy)
 		return ret;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The regulator core counts this policy's voltage only once its OPP has
+	 * been set, so on a supply shared with another policy or device the
+	 * rail could drop below what this CPU's current clock needs. Set the OPP
+	 * now, at the current clock; an unlisted boot rate moves to the next
+	 * OPP up, where a failure can still be returned.
+	 */
+	if (priv->opp_token > 0) {
+		ret = dev_pm_opp_set_rate(cpu_dev, clk_get_rate(cpu_clk));
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(cpu_dev, "failed to set the initial OPP: %d\n", ret);
+			clk_put(cpu_clk);
+			return ret;
+		}
+	}
+
 	transition_latency = dev_pm_opp_get_max_transition_latency(cpu_dev);
 	if (!transition_latency)
 		transition_latency = CPUFREQ_DEFAULT_TRANSITION_LATENCY_NS;
-- 
